ref 17592186045417] [17592186100186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "94d4c6ac-6a98-5ca6-aacc-c8c91c7c2181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100187 #edb/ref 1080 "$$176000$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100187 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100187 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b95f6bfc-f019-590c-a20a-055d06c04ab2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100188 #edb/ref 1080 "$$107000$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100188 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100188 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2031fd6c-5f54-5518-bdfb-161c34ad9c95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100189 #edb/ref 1080 "$$310000$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100189 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100189 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38745beb-e99b-5af8-8495-9f307871a20b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100190 #edb/ref 1080 "$$243000$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100190 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100190 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69ab0c33-8f37-5a7e-960b-d2e068a98b8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100545 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "66e4fa29-0429-5e20-b824-7bf28e1113c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1080 "$$850$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc079cff-d7da-5a87-b324-3586bd45da20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1080 "$$640$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100547 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0a0efd8-50ec-59dd-9e81-0c4bfa6eebc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1080 "$$483.5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100548 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d055eaa7-a9f5-5d9e-932a-3b0feca2a7ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100549 #edb/ref 1080 "$$1000$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d56e880d-b013-5367-8a35-f97835b2241d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1080 "$$219.7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8616558-a163-56ed-b4e0-530b461b2845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1069 "e-357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1071 "A company spends $C(x) = x^2 + 80x$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $\\[math] 180$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $60$ units of product per month?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c44b63d-72f8-599a-a89c-29fdc2227ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100660 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of\n\n$$\nR(x)=180\\cdot x=180 x\n$$\n\ndollars.\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= 180x - (x^{2} + 80x) \\\\\n&= 100x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the question statement, we know that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\leq 60\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 60$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x)=100-2x\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n100 - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=50$ lies in the interval $0 < x < 60$. All that's left now is to confirm that $x=50$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=50$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=50$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(50) &= 100(50) - (50)^{2} = \\[math]2500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1069 "e-359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1071 "The director of a newspaper estimates that the company's profit, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n$P(x) = -x^2 + 44x + 5$,\nwhere $x$ is the number of newspapers, in thousands, produced daily. Given that the company has the capacity to produce $30\\,000$ newspapers every day, how many newspapers per day should the company produce to maximize profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e47dca19-6772-532a-9955-ebe6f3db0d28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100661 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we note that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30\n$$\n\nsince $x = 30$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n30 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 30\\,000\n$$\n\ncopies of the newspaper. Therefore, $x=0$ and $x = 30$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 30$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = -2x + 44\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n- 2x + 44 &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= 44 \\\\\nx &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=22$ lies in the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=22$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=22$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=22$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the company should produce\n\n$$\n22 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 22\\,000\n$$\n\ncopies of the newspaper per day to maximize its profit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1071 "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100784 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (21 - x)_(⏟)_(occupied rooms) \\cdot (12 + x)_(⏟)_(rate per room) \\\\\n&= 252 + 21x - 12x - x^{2} \\\\\n&= 252 + 9x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= 3(21 - x) \\\\\n&= 63 - 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\nP(x) &= (252 + 9x - x^{2}) - (63 - 3x) \\\\\n&= 189 + 12x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x\\leq 21\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=21$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n12 - 2x &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 < x < 21$. All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.\n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 6 &= \\[math]18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1071 "A textile center sells $x$ hundreds of meters of fabric at a price $p(x)$, in dollars, given by\n$p(x) = x^2 - 15x + 48 + \\dfrac{78}{x}$.\n\nIf the textile center sells between $100$ and $900$ meters of fabric daily, how many meters of fabric must the textile center sell to maximize its revenue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7996afa5-da25-51e5-915c-5f0acdccb8bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100788 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ hundreds of meters in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x(x^{2} - 15x + 48 + \\frac{78}{x}) \\\\\n&= x^{3} - 15x^{2} + 48x + 78\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 9\n$$\n\nsince $x = 1$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot 100 = 100\n$$\n\nand $x = 9$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n9 \\cdot 100 = 900\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ and $x=9$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1 < x < 9$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nR'(x) = 3x^2 - 30x + 48\n$$\n\nand solving $R'(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 30x + 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x - 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and $x=8$.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,2, 8$ and $9$. Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(1) &= (1)^{3} - 15(1)^{2} + 48(1) + 78 = 112 \\\\\nR(2) &= (2)^{3} - 15(2)^{2} + 48(2) + 78 = 122✓ \\\\\nR(8) &= (8)^{3} - 15(8)^{2} + 48(8) + 78 = 14 \\\\\nR(9) &= (9)^{3} - 15(9)^{2} + 48(9) + 78 = 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the textile center should sell\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 100 = 200\n$$\n\nmeters of fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1039 "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101017 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101018 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101019 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101020 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimization Problems in Economics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101072 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0e211b6d-7f83-5173-9b92-9e29afdbbe93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1071 "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300$. The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101113 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory spends $C(x) = x^{4} + 13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5c03e3f-902a-5f23-a083-877f778bea41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101504 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1071 "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200$. The cost of providing the course is $C(x) = 200x - 4x^{3} + 0.3x^{4}$, where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5396c61-6040-5997-8775-94633d636485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101564 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1061 606252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fc21a4d-0b82-5f3e-b8d9-126d7179656f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1061 606253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822eb919-1779-5cf4-9c9e-2c66f15e9e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1061 606256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f61e403-b029-5b57-97a2-0449bc4a4ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1061 606257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8f2b97-af90-5292-ad79-44e12e42010b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1061 606260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5614397-09dd-526a-abea-3acccf854d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1061 606266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca0f41cd-a2e3-5d80-9d45-4b2c58377bc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good.\n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= (fixed cost) + (number of units) \\cdot (cost per unit) \\\\\n&= (fixed cost) + x \\cdot (cost per unit)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company.\n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x)$, then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1065 4663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb6a794d-d60c-5d16-8912-b69af938c892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101994 #edb/ref 1067 "A Review of Price and Cost Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1064 "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. The candidates test gives them a method for doing this. Let's find out how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1065 682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d29b308-bacd-5426-b6c8-43b0da8fcd75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
